<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/2nd-century-bce">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Achaean War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/146-bce">146 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Achaean War of 146 BCE was the conflict in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> crushed the Achaean League and its allies in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>. After the league defied Roman demands, the consul <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucius-mummius-achaicus">Lucius Mummius</a> defeated its army near Corinth and sacked the city. Greece then came under Roman control, and cities that had supported the Achaeans, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalcis">Chalcis</a> and Megalopolis, lost their independence.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/achaean-war.jpg" alt="Achaean War" width="320" height="219" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sailko · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tony_robert-fleury,_l%27ultimo_giorno_di_corinto,_ante_1870.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and the Achaean League, an alliance of Achaean and other Peloponnesian states, had been allies since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-macedonian-war">Second Macedonian War</a>. Relations soured over Roman efforts to check the league's ambitions, above all its wish to absorb <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a>, and over the thousands of hostages Rome took during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-macedonian-war">Third Macedonian War</a>, whose return the league sought through at least five embassies. Achaean politics divided between the followers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philopoemen">Philopoemen</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lycortas">Lycortas</a>, who wanted the league to set its own foreign policy, and those of Aristaenus and Diophanes, who would yield to Rome.</p><p>In 148 BCE the strategos <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diaeus">Diaeus</a> subjugated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a>, which had revolted. In 147 BCE a Roman embassy under Lucius Aurelius Orestes announced that the league was to be reduced to its original, narrow grouping and was almost mobbed. When a further mission under Sextus Julius Caesar failed, the Achaeans declared war on Sparta and elected Critolaos as strategos; historians debate whether this was also an implicit declaration against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Two Roman armies moved against the league: one under the consul <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucius-mummius-achaicus">Lucius Mummius</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, the other under the praetor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quintus-caecilius-metellus-macedonicus">Quintus Caecilius Metellus Macedonicus</a>, who had just put down the rising of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andriscus">Andriscus</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macedonia-roman-province">Macedonia</a>. Metellus caught Critolaos at Scarpheia and defeated him; Critolaos died during or after the battle. Elis and Messene surrendered, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diaeus">Diaeus</a> was elected in his place and fought on, arresting or killing those who favoured peace.</p><p>Mummius gathered 23,000 infantry and 3,500 cavalry against the 13,500 infantry and 650 cavalry of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diaeus">Diaeus</a>. Outside Corinth the Roman cavalry drove off the Achaean horse, and 1,000 picked Roman infantry routed the exposed army. Diaeus fled to Megalopolis, where he killed his wife and then himself.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> entered Corinth three days later, massacred its adult men, enslaved the women and children and destroyed the city, in the same year as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a>. Mommsen explained this as the Senate's instruction, urged by those eager to remove a commercial rival; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polybius">Polybius</a> wrote that Mummius could not resist the pressure of those around him. Leagues were dissolved, walls torn down and tribute imposed, and the Greek states were placed in the province of Macedonia; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaea">Achaea</a> became a province of its own under Augustus in 27 BCE. Mummius held a triumph and took the name Achaicus, and the flow of Greek art and slaves to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> hastened the growth of a Greco-Roman culture.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Achaean War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the <b>Achaean War</b> of 146 BCE Greece came under the oversight of the Roman governors of Macedonia, and Delphi's freedom became a privilege granted by Rome.</blockquote><a href="/empire/delphi">Delphi · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Sparta's attempts to leave the league helped provoke the <b>Achaean War</b>, and after Rome destroyed Corinth in 146 BCE Sparta became a free city allied to Rome.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sparta">Sparta · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>After the dissolution of the Boeotian League following the <b>Achaean War</b> of 146 BC, Thebes came under Roman rule, and in 27 BC it was included in the Province of Achaia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/thebes">Thebes · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>It lost its independence after the <b>Achaean War</b> of 146 BCE, when Chalcis was punished for supporting the Achaeans.</blockquote><a href="/state/euboean-league">Euboean League</a></li><li><blockquote>It lost independence after the <b>Achaean War</b> of 146 BCE and declined under Roman rule; Strabo called it a great desert.</blockquote><a href="/state/megalopolis">Megalopolis</a></li><li><blockquote>After the <b>Achaean War</b> in 146 BCE it passed under Roman rule.</blockquote><a href="/state/messene">Messene</a></li></ul></section>
